// Congruence closure decides the theory of ground equations. What about identities?

== Equality saturation
If we have $forall x ..., t = s$, try looking for *instances* of this in
the E-graph.

If we find an instance, _we might have to add the other side_ into the E-graph, if it
is not yet present. Then, we merge the corresponding E-classes.

// #set align(left)
// == Equality saturation
// Loop until nothing changes:
// - E-match
// - Insert (if needed)
// - Merge

// == Equality saturation
// This doesn't always terminate

// Congruence closure only ever shrinks the E-graph, by bringing together E-classes

// Insertion will grow the E-graph

// == Equality saturation
// Still, it terminates in many cases, or we can use heuristics to decide when to stop

// == E-graph refresher: equality saturation

// Idea: if we have , try instantiating the quantifier with known E-classes.

// _Surely_ if we have some equational proof we want to find, it will involve identities instantiated at ground terms in this obvious way.

// #pause

// The truth is a little more subtle, but I will gloss over this.

// == Equality saturation

// After instantiation, we have a ground equation $t' = s'$.

// We can just insert this into the graph and run congruence closure/rebuilding.

// == Equality saturation

// Is this complete? Not always. Another complicated story...

// #pause

// But a key takeaway here is that if $t'$ and $s'$ are already represented, equating them _shrinks_ the E-graph.
// But inserting new things can grow the E-graph arbitrarily.


#set align(center)
== E-graphs vs AC
Or really, equality saturation vs AC

A: associativity

$f(x, f(y, z)) = f(f(x, y), z)$

C: commutativity

$f(x, y) = f(y, x)$

AC: both

== E-graphs vs AC
AC is the bane of equality saturation (or AC + other stuff, ACU, abelian groups, etc.).

We will use '+' as our example AC-operator.

