== Some insights from rewriting
E-graphs are just certain kinds of rewrite systems

== E-graphs as a rewrite system
#slide(composer: (1fr, auto))[
  #figure(
    image("figures/exampleBasic5.svg", width: 65%),
  )
][
  $
    a → e_a \
    b → e_b \
    c → e_c \
    g(e_a) → e_1 \
    g(e_b) → e_2 \
    g(e_c) → e_2 \
    ...
  $
]

== E-graphs as a rewrite system
#slide(composer: (1fr, auto))[
  #figure(
    image("figures/exampleBasic5.svg", width: 65%),
  )
][
  $
    f(e_1, e_c) → e_3 \
    h(e_a, e_2) → e_3 \
    h(e_a, e_2) → e_4 \
    h(e_2, e_b) → e_4
  $
]

== What congruence closure is doing
$e_3 ← h(e_a, e_2) → e_4$

When we can rewrite in two different ways, we call it a *critical pair*.

*Resolve* critical pairs by merging: in this case, $e_3$ and $e_4$

== AC closure
We need to find AC-critical pairs

$c + b ← (a+a)+b =_{A C} a+(a+b) → a + b$

So, we learn that $c + b = a + b$

#set align(left)
== AC closure
Loop:
- Find an AC-critical pair $t_1 ← s → t_2$
- Take the equation $t_1 = t_2$ and *orient* it into a new AC-rewrite rule $t_1 → t_2$ or $t_2 → t_1$
- Interreduce the new rule with the old ones

== Integrating theories and E-graphs: design choices
There are two ways to think about adding an AC-solver to an E-graph.

1. 'Integrated': Treat +-nodes as any other E-graph node, and scan for critical pairs between them
2. 'Side-table': Don't store +-nodes in the normal E-graph at all. Instead, have a side table of AC-rewriting rules.
  - When you add e.g. $e_1 + e_2$ to the E-graph, create a new rewrite $e_1 + e_2 → e_3$ for some fresh $e_3$
  - Whenever we discover a rewrite $e_j → e_k$ in the AC-solver, feed it back to the original E-graph

== Integrating theories and E-graphs
I chose option 2 for my current implementation. Indeed, I didn't even think too hard about the tradeoff,
as it seemed obvious that these are equivalent in some sense.

== The integrated approach
We could take a discovered equation $t_1 = t_2$ and introduce a new E-class id $e$ and add

$
  t_1 → e \
  t_2 → e
$

This is more or less the same as the side-table approach (I intent to give a proof in my thesis) and a bit more "E-graph like", since
we only ever have $f(...) → e$ and never $f(...) → g(...)$ in ordinary E-graphs.

== Equivalent, but only if you pay attention
However, there is one advantage of the 'side-table' approach that might be accidentally missed in the integrated approach.

In the side-table approach, if we build the rewrite $e_1 + e_2 → e_3$ and we have an existing rule
$e_1 + e_2 + e_2 → e_4$, we can compress the existing rule down to $e_2 + e_3 → e_4$.

This is a standard step in textbook Buchberger's algorithm.

== Equivalent, but only if you pay attention
To make the integrated method equivalent, we need to do some _destructive rewriting_ of the E-graph in a way that is maybe a little alien to the typical
ways of working with E-graphs.

// == Why this terminates
// Consider the AC-term $a + a + b$ that is on the left hand side of some AC-rewrite rule.

// We can think of it as $2a + b$

// Any new term $k_1 a + k_2 b + ...$ with $k_1 ≥ 2, k_2 ≥ 1$ will reduce by this one.

// == Why this terminates
// Every rewrite rule means we won't make new rewrite rules that *dominate* it, by having an LHS greater than its LHS in every term: those would get reduced by it.

// You can only add so many rewrite rules that aren't dominated by previous ones

